.TH LS 1 "September 19, 2026" maux
.SH NAME
ls \- list files and directories
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B ls
.RB [ \-lC1trdFRs ]
.RI [ path ...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Ls
lists the contents of one or more directories under control of several
options. If no argument is given, the current directory is listed.
If an argument is not a directory, its name is given.
The normal order is by name.
.TP
.B \-l
List in long format: ProDOS type, size, modification date and name.
There are no Unix ownership or rwx columns. A device entry under
.I /dev
shows BLK or CHR in the type column instead of a ProDOS file type.
.TP
.B \-t
Sort by time modified, latest first.
.TP
.B \-r
Reverse the selected order.
.TP
.B \-s
Give allocated 512-byte blocks for each entry.
.TP
.B \-d
List a directory itself, not its contents.
.TP
.B \-F
Append / to directory names.
.TP
.B \-R
Recursively list subdirectories in depth-first name order.
.TP
.B \-C, \-1
Force columns or one entry per line.
.PP
ProDOS has no ordinary dot-file names needing a hidden-file option.
.PP
.I /
and
.I /dev
are synthetic directories with no ProDOS file behind them: the root
lists every online volume (never a 5.25-inch drive -- listing it never
polls one), and
.I /dev
lists the fixed device names (tty, ttya0, null) plus one sNdM entry
per online unit, including floppies.
.SH FILES
/usr/man/LS  this page
.SH "SEE ALSO"
file(1), du(1), chmod(1), licenses(1)
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
Exit status is 0 if all operands were listed, 1 for access errors or a truncated listing, 2 for usage errors.
.SH BUGS
A directory listing holds at most 128 entries; truncation is reported. Recursive listing allows at most sixteen pending directory paths. Unknown clock stamps appear as '-' rather than invented dates.
